<title>USA: U.S. Feb consumer prices rose 0.3 pct. [CORRECTED 14:55 GMT]</title>
<headline>U.S. Feb consumer prices rose 0.3 pct. [CORRECTED 14:55 GMT]</headline>
<dateline>WASHINGTON 1997-03-19</dateline>
<text>
<p>U.S. Labor Department monthly Consumer Price Index (CPI-U), 1982-84 equals 100:</p>
<p>    Percent Changes:  Seasonally Adj.    Unadjusted</p>
<p>    -			   Feb    Jan     Feb97/96</p>
<p> All Items		    0.3    0.1	3.0</p>
<p> -Excluding Food/Energy   0.2    0.1	2.5</p>
<p> Energy			 0.3    0.8	7.8</p>
<p> Food and Beverages	 0.3   -0.2	3.7</p>
<p> Food			   0.3   -0.3	3.8</p>
<p> CPI-Urban Consumers-X  159.6  159.1	N/A</p>
<p>(Corrects Feb 97 seasonally adjusted figure to 159.6 from 156.6)</p>
<p>X-Data unadjusted. N/A-Not Available.  </p>
<p>    Percent Changes:  Seasonally Adj.    Unadjusted</p>
<p>    -			   Feb    Jan     Feb97/96</p>
<p> Food at Home		 0.5   -0.6	4.2</p>
<p> -Beef and Veal	    -0.8   -0.5	1.3</p>
<p> -Pork			  0.1    0.3     10.9</p>
<p> -Poultry (Unadjusted)   -0.2    0.3	5.7</p>
<p> -Fresh Fruits		1.3   -5.3	5.8</p>
<p> -Fresh Vegetables	  8.9   -0.1	5.4</p>
<p> --Tomatoes (Unadjusted)  9.3    0.1     18.9</p>
<p> --Lettuce (Unadjusted)  -5.8    6.1     11.4</p>
<p>    Percent Changes:  Seasonally Adj.    Unadjusted</p>
<p>    -			   Feb    Jan     Feb97/96</p>
<p> Services		     0.2    0.2	3.2</p>
<p> Housing			0.3    0.3	3.0</p>
<p> -Home Fuel Oil	    -1.9   -1.2     12.2</p>
<p> -Homeowner Costs-Y	 0.3    0.2	2.8</p>
<p> Apparel, Upkeep	    0.2    0.4	0.5</p>
<p> Transportation	    -0.1   -0.2	3.1</p>
<p> -Gasoline		   Unch    0.7	9.8</p>
<p> -New Cars		   Unch   -0.2	1.1</p>
<p>Y-Dec 1982=100 base.  </p>
<p>    Percent Changes:  Seasonally Adj.    Unadjusted</p>
<p>    -			   Feb    Jan     Feb97/96</p>
<p> Medical Care		 0.2    0.2	2.9</p>
<p> Commodities		  0.2   Unch	2.8</p>
<p> CPI-W			  0.2    0.1	3.0</p>
<p>CPI-W--Index for urban wage earners and clerical workers.</p>
<p>Wall Street economists expected a 0.2 percent rise in February consumer prices. Excluding food and energy, they also expected a 0.2 percent increase.</p>
<p>Labor said the price of tobacco and smoking products rose 0.6 percent in February, and airline fares fell 2.6 percent.</p>
